\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Temporal Geometry of Entropy Evolution\par
Joseph Jackson  \par
Independent Researcher\par
Townsend, TN, USA\par
Email: plantshaman2012@hotmail.com\par
ORCID: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://orcid.org/0009-0007-3664-5942 }}{\fldrslt{https://orcid.org/0009-0007-3664-5942\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22\par
\par
Abstract\par
\par
Entropy is traditionally evaluated through statistical randomness, yet a deeper structure emerges when entropy is examined as a temporal process rather than a static distribution. Across more than a dozen datasets spanning hardware entropy, atmospheric entropy, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, and raw temporal dynamics, we find that entropy sources exhibit stable, reproducible temporal geometry. Using a resolution\'e2\'80\lquote invariant computational pipeline applied to hexagram, byte, 16\'e2\'80\lquote bit, 64\'e2\'80\lquote bit, and unclustered manifolds, we observe consistent oscillatory behavior, curvature signatures, basin formation, attractor structure, and drift hierarchies independent of encoding, resolution, or device.\par
\par
Hardware entropy displays moderate curvature and basin depth; atmospheric entropy remains flat with zero curvature; symbolic encodings amplify curvature; inverted encodings reflect manifold symmetry; raw temporal dynamics reveal oscillation without clustering; and PRNG\'e2\'80\lquote based probes demonstrate clean, noise\'e2\'80\lquote free temporal drift. These behaviors persist across all clustering resolutions (k = 8 - 128) and across all manifold transformations, establishing resolution invariance and manifold independence.\par
\par
The results demonstrate that temporal geometry is a universal property of entropy evolution. Symbolic and numeric manifolds function as temporal filters, enabling classification of entropy sources by temporal behavior rather than statistical randomness. This work provides the first multi\'e2\'80\lquote manifold, multi\'e2\'80\lquote entropy empirical evidence that temporal geometry is measurable, reproducible, and computationally accessible, establishing it as a new domain of scientific inquiry.\par
\par
Keywords\par
Temporal geometry, entropy evolution, tunneling entropy, symbolic manifold, stochastic drift, computational physics\par
\par
1. Introduction\par
\par
Entropy is usually treated as a static property measured through distributions, randomness tests, or statistical uniformity. But entropy is not static. It unfolds. It moves. It evolves over time. And when entropy is examined as a temporal process rather than a purely statistical one, a deeper structure emerges: patterns of drift, curvature, oscillation, basin formation, and attractor behavior that remain invisible to conventional randomness analysis. This work introduces and formalizes temporal geometry, the study of how entropy behaves as a dynamical system.\par
\par
The foundation of temporal geometry is the observation that entropy sources produce distinct, reproducible temporal fingerprints when mapped into symbolic or numeric manifolds. Early experiments using the hexagram manifold revealed measurable curvature, basin depth, and drift hierarchy across hardware, atmospheric, symbolic, and inverted symbolic entropy sources. These findings suggested that symbolic manifolds act as temporal filters or structures that reveal the underlying temporal behavior of entropy rather than its statistical randomness.\par
\par
Over the course of extended experimentation, the scope of this work expanded dramatically. New manifolds were introduced: byte, 16\f1\_\f0 bit, 64\f1\_\f0 bit, and raw temporal dynamics without clustering. New entropy sources were added: multiple hardware devices, atmospheric RNG, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, and high\f1\_\f0 resolution Android entropy probes. Across more than a dozen datasets, one phenomenon appeared universally: temporal oscillation. Regardless of device, encoding, resolution, or manifold, entropy exhibited stable, repeating patterns of temporal drift.\par
\par
This paper consolidates the full body of temporal\f1\_\f0 geometry research into a single scientific report. It presents the computational pipeline used to analyze entropy sources across multiple manifolds and resolutions; demonstrates resolution invariance and manifold independence; establishes curvature, drift, and basin metrics as reliable temporal descriptors; and provides empirical evidence that temporal geometry is a real, measurable, reproducible domain.\par
\par
The central thesis of this work is that entropy contains temporal structure, and that symbolic and numeric manifolds reveal that structure. Temporal geometry enables classification of entropy sources by their temporal behavior rather than their statistical randomness. The discovery of universal oscillation across all datasets suggests that temporal geometry is not an artifact of encoding or measurement but rather a fundamental property of entropy evolution.\par
\par
2. Materials and Methods\par
\par
The methodological framework for this study is designed to measure temporal behavior in entropy without depending on encoding, resolution, device, or manifold. To accomplish this, a resolution invariant and manifold independent computational pipeline was developed and applied to a wide range of entropy sources. These sources include hardware entropy from multiple machines, atmospheric entropy from Random.org, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift captured through the Temporal Thermometer\u8239?2.0 Android probe, and raw temporal dynamics that were analyzed without clustering. Each dataset represents a distinct modality and was processed independently before being compared across manifolds to evaluate temporal geometry invariance.\par
\par
Tunneling entropy was collected using a custom LED electron tunneling noise generator designed specifically for this study. The circuit consists of a dual LM358P operational amplifier powered by a 5\u8239?V USB input. A 4.7\u8239?k\f2\lang1032\'d9 bias resistor and a 0.1\f0\lang1033\u8239?\'b5F coupling capacitor form the tunneling junction across a clear epoxy LED, producing stochastic electron tunneling events that generate a broadband noise signal. This signal is amplified through the LM358P and coupled via a 0.1\u8239?\'b5F output capacitor to a 3.5\u8239?mm stereo audio interface connected to a Sabrent USB sound card. The schematic and physical layout are shown in Figure\u8239?1. The probe was mounted on a foam block with female\f1\_\f0 female jumper wires and DIP\f1\_\f0 8 breadboard adapters to ensure mechanical stability and electrical isolation. The resulting analog signal was digitized at 48\u8239?kHz and recorded as raw entropy data for subsequent temporal\f1\_\f0 geometry analysis. This device served as the primary hardware entropy source for the tunneling datasets used in this study.\par
