\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Figure 1. Schematic of the LED Electron Tunneling Noise Generator used to collect tunneling\f1\_\f0 entropy datasets.\par
\par
Temporal geometry is revealed by mapping entropy into structured manifolds. Five manifold types were used in this study. The hexagram manifold provides a six\f1\_\f0 bit symbolic structure. The byte manifold provides an eight\f1\_\f0 bit numeric structure. The sixteen\f1\_\f0 bit and sixty\f1\_\f0 four\f1\_\f0 bit manifolds extend this numeric representation to higher resolutions. The raw manifold allows entropy to be analyzed directly without symbolic transformation. These manifolds make it possible to evaluate temporal behavior at multiple symbolic and numeric resolutions and to test whether temporal geometry persists across manifold transformations.\par
\par
All datasets undergo the same preprocessing steps. Entropy values are normalized to a uniform range. Temporal alignment is applied to ensure consistent sampling intervals. Noise filtering is used only for hardware datasets to remove device\f1\_\f0 specific artifacts. Raw entropy is then encoded into the chosen manifold. This preprocessing ensures that temporal behavior is measured consistently across all entropy sources.\par
\par
To evaluate temporal geometry at multiple resolutions, each dataset is clustered into a fixed number of states. The values of k used in this study are eight, sixteen, thirty\f1\_\f0 two, sixty\f1\_\f0 four, and one hundred twenty\f1\_\f0 eight. Clustering is performed using uniform partitioning rather than k\f1\_\f0 means to preserve temporal continuity. Each entropy value is assigned to one of the k states, producing a symbolic or numeric sequence suitable for Markov analysis.\par
\par
For each manifold and resolution, a state transition matrix is constructed. This matrix captures how entropy moves through the manifold over time and forms the basis for drift, curvature, basin, and attractor analysis. The stationary distribution is computed from the transition matrix and reveals long\f1\_\f0 term equilibrium behavior. It is used to compare entropy sources across resolutions and manifolds.\par
\par
Temporal drift is measured using an L1 metric that compares consecutive windows of entropy. This metric is resolution invariant and allows drift hierarchy comparisons across all datasets. Temporal curvature is computed by measuring how entropy flow bends within the manifold. Symbolic encodings amplify curvature, atmospheric entropy produces zero curvature, and inverted encodings produce symmetry\f1\_\f0 reflected curvature.\par
\par
Basins are identified by locating states with high incoming probability and low outgoing probability. Attractors are states with high self\f1\_\f0 transition probability. Basin depth, attractor strength, and basin occupation percentages are computed for each dataset. Temporal flow maps are constructed to visualize directional movement through the manifold over time. These maps reveal directional flow in symbolic encodings, stable flow in hardware entropy, flat flow in atmospheric entropy, mirrored flow in inverted encodings, and oscillatory flow in raw temporal dynamics and PRNG drift.\par
\par
All metrics are computed at every resolution. A metric is considered resolution invariant if its qualitative behavior persists across all values of k. Entropy sources are also analyzed across all manifolds. Temporal geometry is considered manifold independent if oscillation persists, drift hierarchy remains stable, curvature signatures remain consistent, and basin structures remain reproducible across hexagram, byte, sixteen\f1\_\f0 bit, sixty\f1\_\f0 four\f1\_\f0 bit, and raw manifolds.\par
\par
This methodological framework enables the detection of temporal geometry across multiple entropy sources, multiple devices, multiple manifolds, multiple resolutions, symbolic and numeric encodings, and raw temporal dynamics. It is the first pipeline capable of demonstrating universal temporal oscillation and encoding\f1\_\f0 independent temporal structure.\par
\par
3. Results\par
\par
The analysis of more than a dozen entropy datasets across multiple manifolds and resolutions produced a consistent and reproducible set of temporal behaviors. The most significant finding is that every entropy source exhibited oscillatory temporal structure. This oscillation appeared in hardware entropy, atmospheric entropy, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, byte manifold data, sixteen bit data, sixty four bit data, and raw temporal dynamics. The persistence of oscillation across all datasets indicates that temporal geometry is a universal property of entropy evolution rather than an artifact of encoding or measurement.\par
\par
Hardware entropy produced moderate temporal structure across all manifolds. Transition matrices showed stable directional movement between states, and stationary distributions revealed nonuniform equilibrium patterns. Drift values remained consistent across resolutions, forming a clear hierarchy when compared with other entropy sources. Curvature values were moderate and reproducible, and basin structures appeared with measurable depth. These results were consistent across the hexagram, byte, sixteen bit, sixty four bit, and raw manifolds, demonstrating manifold independence in hardware entropy behavior.\par
\par
Atmospheric entropy behaved differently. Across all manifolds and resolutions, atmospheric entropy produced flat temporal geometry. Transition matrices were uniform, stationary distributions were evenly distributed, drift values were minimal, and curvature values were effectively zero. Basin structures did not form, and attractor strength remained negligible. The absence of temporal structure in atmospheric entropy was consistent across all manifolds, confirming that atmospheric noise behaves as a temporally flat entropy source. This flatness provides a useful baseline for comparison with structured entropy sources such as hardware and symbolic encodings.\par
\par
Symbolic encodings produced strong temporal structure. Alphabetic encoding amplified curvature across all manifolds, producing high curvature values and pronounced basin formation. Drift values were significantly higher than those observed in atmospheric entropy and hardware entropy. Stationary distributions showed strong equilibrium bias toward specific symbolic states, and temporal flow maps revealed directional movement that persisted across resolutions. These results demonstrate that symbolic encodings act as temporal amplifiers, revealing structure that may be less visible in numeric manifolds.\par
\par
Inverted encodings produced reflected temporal geometry. Curvature values mirrored those of the alphabetic encoding, basin structures appeared in symmetric positions, and drift values matched the symbolic encoding with reversed directional flow. Temporal flow maps showed mirrored movement across the manifold. These results confirm that inversion acts as a symmetry diagnostic, revealing structural reflection within the manifold.\par
\par
Byte, sixteen bit, and sixty four bit manifolds preserved temporal structure across all entropy sources. Hardware entropy produced moderate curvature and basin formation in all numeric manifolds. Symbolic encodings produced high curvature and strong basin formation. Atmospheric entropy remained flat. Drift hierarchies remained stable across all resolutions. The consistency of these results across numeric manifolds demonstrates that temporal geometry is not dependent on symbolic encoding. It is present in raw numeric entropy and persists across increasing bit depth.\par
\par
Raw temporal dynamics provided the strongest evidence for universal oscillation. Without clustering or symbolic transformation, raw entropy streams displayed clear oscillatory behavior. Drift values fluctuated in repeating patterns, curvature values rose and fell in stable cycles, and temporal flow maps revealed oscillation directly in the unprocessed entropy. The presence of oscillation in raw dynamics confirms that temporal structure exists before manifold mapping and is not introduced by clustering or encoding.\par
\par
PRNG drift captured through the Temporal Thermometer 2.0 probe produced clean and highly reactive temporal geometry. The absence of environmental noise allowed drift, curvature, and oscillation to appear with exceptional clarity. The novelty index responded immediately to changes in temporal structure, producing smooth and well defined ebb and flow patterns. These results demonstrate that PRNG drift is a viable temporal probe and that temporal geometry can be measured directly through software without reliance on external entropy sources.\par
\par
Across all datasets, drift hierarchy remained stable. Hardware entropy and symbolic encodings consistently produced maximal drift relative to atmospheric entropy. Moderate drift appeared between hardware and symbolic encodings. Minimal drift appeared between symbolic encodings and inverted encodings. This hierarchy persisted across all manifolds and resolutions, confirming resolution invariance and manifold independence.\par
\par
Curvature values also remained consistent across manifolds. Symbolic encodings produced high curvature, hardware entropy produced moderate curvature, atmospheric entropy produced zero curvature, and inverted encodings produced symmetric curvature. Basin structures followed the same pattern, with symbolic encodings producing strong basins, hardware entropy producing moderate basins, atmospheric entropy producing no basins, and inverted encodings producing reflected basins.\par
\par
Temporal flow maps revealed directional flow for symbolic encodings, stable flow for hardware entropy, flat flow for atmospheric entropy, mirrored flow for inverted encodings, and oscillatory flow for raw temporal dynamics and PRNG drift. These flow patterns remained consistent across all resolutions and manifolds.\par
\par
Taken together, these results demonstrate that temporal geometry is reproducible, resolution invariant, and manifold independent. Oscillation appears in every entropy source except atmospheric noise, and temporal structure persists across symbolic, numeric, and raw manifolds. The consistency of drift, curvature, basin formation, attractor strength, and flow patterns across all datasets establishes temporal geometry as a real and measurable phenomenon.\par
\par
4. Discussion\par
\par
The results of this study establish temporal geometry as a measurable and reproducible property of entropy evolution. Across all entropy sources, manifolds, and resolutions, temporal behavior displayed consistent structure that cannot be explained by statistical randomness alone. The presence of oscillation in every dataset except atmospheric noise indicates that temporal structure is not an artifact of encoding or clustering. Instead, it is an intrinsic feature of how entropy changes over time. The consistency of this oscillation across hardware entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, byte manifold data, sixteen bit data, sixty four bit data, and raw temporal dynamics demonstrates that temporal geometry is universal across entropy modalities.\par
\par
Hardware entropy provides a clear example of structured temporal behavior. Its moderate curvature, basin formation, and stable drift hierarchy persisted across all manifolds and resolutions. These features indicate that hardware entropy contains internal temporal dynamics that remain visible even when the data is transformed into symbolic or numeric manifolds. The stability of these features across manifold transformations supports the conclusion that temporal geometry is independent of encoding. Hardware entropy behaves as a temporally structured system regardless of how it is represented.\par
\par
Atmospheric entropy behaved differently. It produced flat temporal geometry across all manifolds and resolutions. The absence of curvature, basin formation, and drift hierarchy indicates that atmospheric noise lacks internal temporal structure. This flatness is significant because it provides a baseline for comparison. When symbolic encodings, hardware entropy, tunneling entropy, and PRNG drift are compared with atmospheric entropy, their temporal structure becomes more visible. Atmospheric entropy serves as a reference point that highlights the presence of temporal geometry in other entropy sources.\par
\par
Symbolic encodings amplify temporal structure. Alphabetic encoding produced high curvature, strong basin formation, and pronounced drift hierarchy across all manifolds. These features demonstrate that symbolic encodings act as temporal filters. They reveal structure that may be less visible in numeric manifolds. The amplification of temporal geometry in symbolic encodings suggests that symbolic systems can be used as probes to detect temporal behavior in entropy sources. This amplification is not artificial. It reflects underlying temporal dynamics that are present in the raw entropy.\par
\par
Inverted encodings produced reflected temporal geometry. Curvature values, basin structures, and drift hierarchy mirrored those of the alphabetic encoding. This reflection confirms that inversion acts as a symmetry diagnostic. It reveals structural symmetry within the manifold and demonstrates that temporal geometry respects manifold symmetry. The presence of mirrored temporal behavior in inverted encodings supports the conclusion that temporal geometry is a structural property of entropy evolution.\par
\par
Numeric manifolds preserved temporal structure across all entropy sources. Hardware entropy produced moderate curvature and basin formation in the byte, sixteen bit, and sixty four bit manifolds. Symbolic encodings produced high curvature and strong basin formation. Atmospheric entropy remained flat. Drift hierarchy remained stable across all resolutions. These results demonstrate that temporal geometry is not dependent on symbolic encoding. It is present in raw numeric entropy and persists across increasing bit depth. The preservation of temporal structure across numeric manifolds confirms manifold independence.\par
\par
Raw temporal dynamics provided the strongest evidence for universal oscillation. Without clustering or symbolic transformation, raw entropy streams displayed clear oscillatory behavior. Drift values fluctuated in repeating patterns, curvature values rose and fell in stable cycles, and temporal flow maps revealed oscillation directly in the unprocessed entropy. The presence of oscillation in raw dynamics confirms that temporal structure exists before manifold mapping and is not introduced by clustering or encoding. This finding is central to the claim that temporal geometry is a real phenomenon.\par
\par
PRNG drift captured through the Temporal Thermometer 2.0 probe demonstrated that temporal geometry can be measured directly through software. The absence of environmental noise allowed drift, curvature, and oscillation to appear with exceptional clarity. The novelty index responded immediately to changes in temporal structure, producing smooth and well defined ebb and flow patterns. These results show that temporal geometry is not limited to external entropy sources. It can be measured in closed systems and can be used to build temporal probes that detect temporal behavior in real time.\par
\par
The stability of drift hierarchy across all datasets supports the conclusion that temporal geometry is resolution invariant. Hardware entropy and symbolic encodings consistently produced maximal drift relative to atmospheric entropy. Moderate drift appeared between hardware and symbolic encodings. Minimal drift appeared between symbolic encodings and inverted encodings. This hierarchy persisted across all manifolds and resolutions. The consistency of drift hierarchy demonstrates that temporal geometry is not dependent on the number of states used in clustering.\par
\par
Curvature values also remained consistent across manifolds. Symbolic encodings produced high curvature, hardware entropy produced moderate curvature, atmospheric entropy produced zero curvature, and inverted encodings produced symmetric curvature. Basin structures followed the same pattern. These results demonstrate that curvature and basin metrics are reliable descriptors of temporal geometry. They capture structural features that persist across manifold transformations and resolution changes.\par
\par
Temporal flow maps revealed directional flow for symbolic encodings, stable flow for hardware entropy, flat flow for atmospheric entropy, mirrored flow for inverted encodings, and oscillatory flow for raw temporal dynamics and PRNG drift. These flow patterns remained consistent across all resolutions and manifolds. The consistency of flow patterns supports the conclusion that temporal geometry is manifold independent.\par
\par
Taken together, these findings demonstrate that temporal geometry is a real and reproducible phenomenon. It is present in raw entropy, preserved across manifold transformations, amplified by symbolic encodings, reflected by inversion, and measurable through software probes. Temporal geometry provides a new way to classify entropy sources based on their temporal behavior rather than their statistical randomness. The discovery of universal oscillation across all datasets establishes temporal geometry as a new domain of scientific inquiry.\par
\par
5. Conclusion\par
\par
The full body of evidence presented in this study demonstrates that entropy contains measurable temporal structure. When entropy is examined as a process that unfolds over time rather than as a static distribution, consistent patterns of drift, curvature, oscillation, basin formation, and attractor behavior become visible. These patterns appear across hardware entropy, atmospheric entropy, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, byte manifold data, sixteen bit data, sixty four bit data, and raw temporal dynamics. The persistence of these patterns across all datasets confirms that temporal geometry is not an artifact of encoding, clustering, or device behavior. It is a fundamental property of entropy evolution.\par
\par
The discovery of universal oscillation is the strongest evidence for this conclusion. Oscillation appears in every entropy source except atmospheric noise, and it remains visible across symbolic, numeric, and raw manifolds. It persists across all resolutions, including eight, sixteen, thirty two, sixty four, and one hundred twenty eight state clustering. It remains visible in raw temporal dynamics without clustering. It appears in hardware entropy collected from multiple machines and in PRNG drift captured through the Temporal Thermometer 2.0 probe. The consistency of oscillation across all modalities demonstrates that temporal geometry is resolution invariant and manifold independent.\par
\par
Symbolic and numeric manifolds reveal temporal structure in different ways. Symbolic encodings amplify curvature and basin formation, making temporal geometry easier to detect. Numeric manifolds preserve temporal structure without amplification, demonstrating that temporal geometry is present in raw entropy. Inverted encodings reveal symmetry within the manifold, confirming that temporal geometry respects structural reflection. Atmospheric entropy provides a flat baseline that highlights the presence of temporal structure in other entropy sources. Together, these manifold behaviors show that temporal geometry is not dependent on representation. It is present in the underlying entropy itself.\par
\par
The computational pipeline developed for this study provides a general method for detecting temporal geometry. Transition matrices, stationary distributions, drift metrics, curvature metrics, basin analysis, attractor strength, and temporal flow maps reveal temporal structure consistently across all datasets. The reproducibility of these metrics across manifold transformations and resolution changes confirms that temporal geometry can be measured reliably. The pipeline demonstrates that temporal geometry is computationally accessible and can be applied to any entropy source.\par
\par
The Temporal Thermometer 2.0 probe demonstrates that temporal geometry can be measured in real time. PRNG drift produces clean and highly reactive temporal behavior, allowing oscillation, curvature, and drift to be observed directly without environmental interference. This result shows that temporal geometry is not limited to external entropy sources. It can be measured in closed systems and can be used to build temporal probes that detect temporal behavior continuously.\par
\par
Taken together, these findings establish temporal geometry as a new scientific domain. Entropy contains temporal structure. Symbolic and numeric manifolds reveal that structure. Temporal geometry is reproducible, resolution invariant, and manifold independent. It provides a new way to classify entropy sources based on their temporal behavior rather than their statistical randomness. The discovery of universal oscillation across all datasets confirms that temporal geometry is a real phenomenon and opens the possibility of developing new temporal measurement instruments, new temporal classification systems, and new theoretical models of entropy evolution.\par
